# RV32IMA

## Goal

The goal of this project is to understand and verify the RV32IMA RISC-V instruction set architecture and extend the existing RV32I CPU toward an RV32IMA-compatible processor.

The previous project established the RV32I base ISA. This project adds:

- M: Integer Multiplication and Division
- A: Atomic Instructions

---

## Overview

RISC-V is a modular instruction set architecture.

Instead of defining one large instruction set, RISC-V consists of a base ISA combined with optional extensions.

The processor developed in this project targets:

RV32IMA

Which consists of:

RV32I
+ M
+ A

Where:

| Extension | Meaning         | Purpose                             |
|-----------|-----------------|-------------------------------------|
| I         | Integer         | Base integer instruction set        |
| M         | Multiply/Divide | Integer multiplication and division |
| A         | Atomic          | Atomic memory operations            |

The existing CPU already implements the RV32I base architecture.

This project investigates the additional M and A extensions required to move toward RV32IMA.

---

# Why RV32IMA?

The final goal of this project is to build a custom SoC capable of running increasingly complex software, eventually targeting Linux.

A simple RV32I processor is useful for learning and bare-metal programs, but the Linux target requires a more complete RISC-V ISA and software environment.

For this reason, the project target is RV32IMA rather than stopping at RV32I.

The intention is to establish the processor architecture before starting the larger SoC integration.

