# Important Design Decision

The goal of this project is not to make the CPU as complicated as possible.

The goal is to create a processor that is:

Understandable
      +
Verifiable
      +
Synthesizable
      +
Compatible with the intended software environment

The implementation should therefore start with the simplest correct hardware.

Optimization can be performed later if timing or resource usage becomes a problem on the FPGA.

---

# Linux Considerations

RV32IMA is an important step toward the Linux target, but it is not by itself enough to run Linux.

A Linux-capable system also requires things such as:

- CPU
- Memory
- Boot process
- Interrupt support
- Timer
- UART / console
- Memory map
- Software toolchain
 -Device support
- Operating-system support

Therefore:

RV32IMA ≠ Linux

Instead:

RV32IMA
   │
   ▼
SoC
   │
   ▼
Boot software
   │
   ▼
Bare-metal software
   │
   ▼
Linux-capable platform


The exact Linux ISA requirements should also be verified against the specific Linux configuration and software stack used later in the project.

---

# Limitations

At this stage, the processor may still not be capable of running Linux.

The main remaining system-level requirements include:

- Complete SoC integration
- Memory-mapped peripherals
- Interrupt handling
- Timer functionality
- Boot ROM
- Software toolchain
- Linker configuration
- Appropriate memory layout
- UART console
- Linux-compatible platform support

These will be addressed in later projects.

